noi_dung:
{align:justify|Mr. Thai is 67 years old this year, a man of Chaozhou origin. In 1941, when Japan invaded China and his homeland was besieged and life grew harsh, he left home in his youth to earn a living in Thailand. He now lives in central Thailand, making his living by trade. For nine years his legs have been diseased — like an elephant's legs.}
{align:justify|Mr. Thai loved eating goose meat and drinking wine; every evening he consumed a platter of goose and half a jar of wine. Every single day, without fail.}
{align:justify|Ten years ago he joined a tour to northeastern China, where he tasted the world-famous bear's paw — a memory he carried home with longing. Sadly it was only that once, for back in Thailand he never had another chance to savor that precious dish. Even so, Mr. Thai kept his love of goose feet, for their flavor was somewhat similar. From then on he would often drink wine with goose feet to ease his craving for bear's paw.}
{align:justify|Later someone taught him a way of preparing goose feet, claiming it would taste no worse than bear's paw and could even enrich the essence, strengthen the breath, and nourish the spirit — a secret method of ancient kings.}
{align:justify|And so Mr. Thai learned to prepare goose feet. On an iron table ringed all around with a railing, he set a live goose to stand. Then he lit firewood beneath and heated it gradually. As the temperature rose, the iron table slowly grew hot, and the goose, unable to bear it, tucked up one foot. As the heat climbed higher, neither foot could endure it — the bird alternately tucked and released them, as if dancing. It could not flee, for all sides were fenced. When the iron table glowed red-hot, both of the goose's feet hopped frantically — like a raging tempest. It danced for its very life, nothing but dancing and dancing! Then it went wild, charging blindly, its neck stretched to the utmost, uttering pitiful, mournful cries before collapsing. Now its two feet were burned red and swollen — but it was not yet dead. Seeing this, Mr. Thai swiftly chopped the feet off. The goose fainted from pain, revived, then lapsed back into a swoon and died. But by then Mr. Thai cared nothing for that; he only took the goose feet out, washed them clean, and simmered them low with medicinal herbs.}
{align:justify|According to legend, when the goose has "danced" upon the fiery table, all the breath and blood of its body gather down into its feet, while the rest of its flesh turns tasteless, without nourishing value. The iron table's roasting drives the blood of the whole body down to concentrate in the feet — this is called "goose feet refined into elixir," and Mr. Thai ate such "elixir goose feet" for years on end.}
{align:justify|Afterward his legs fell ill — red and swollen exactly like an elephant's. Every joint in his body ached. He saw doctor after doctor and swallowed every Eastern and Western medicine, all to no avail. His legs hurt 24 hours a day, 365 days a year; this torment racked him for six long years. The misery was unbearable. Many times he wanted to kill himself, and only his good wife's restraint and his filial children's comfort kept him dragging out his remaining breath.}
{align:justify|One night he dreamed a flock of footless geese came seeking him, savagely pecking and biting. Waking drenched in sweat, he connected it at once with his sickness — surely tied to his eating of goose feet. For he had locked living geese on an iron table to roast them, watching them "dance" piteously upon the flames. The more he thought, the more his heart pounded and his flesh trembled. Involuntarily he looked down at his own red swollen legs: alas! They looked exactly like goose feet fresh from dancing on the fire before being chopped off!}
{align:justify|It was craving goose feet that had sown this evil cause, and now the wronged spirits of the geese came demanding their debt. Retribution! Retribution! Who would have thought cause and effect could ripen so swiftly? Now conscience awakened: helped by his wife to the front gate, he knelt and repented most sincerely toward high Heaven. He vowed: "From this day I will not kill, I will eat no meat — I vow to keep a clean mouth, to eat vegetarian for life, until death!" His wife beside him vowed to eat vegetarian with him.}
{align:justify|Strange to tell, from the moment he repented toward Heaven and made his vow, his legs no longer swelled and ached. Only their outward shape remained unsightly, and movement stayed difficult. To this day he takes no medicine and seeks no doctor, for he understands this is proof that karmic creditors have come to claim their debt — so why bother with treatment? He willingly keeps these "elephant" legs as evidence, to warn those who come after.}
{align:justify|Do not kill for a moment's pleasure of the palate. Know that food turns to waste the moment it passes the throat — but if you create evil karma, its retribution will pursue you without end.}
{align:justify|As the sages of old said:}
{align:justify|Misfortune and blessing have no gate — we summon them ourselves. The retribution of good and evil follows like shadow after form! If he can, Mr. Thai should do many good deeds and build merit and virtue — for only merit can dissolve grievances, untie karmic knots, and, with karma ended, end the sickness.}
